<BaseLayout
  title="Harness Engineering"
  description="Eric Nolan's evidence-driven work on the systems surrounding AI models: context, memory, tools, governance, orchestration, verification, and bounded automation."
>
  <header class="page-header">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">Research & engineering practice</p>
      <h1>Harness Engineering</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A working framework for designing and evaluating the systems around AI models: context, memory, instructions, skills, tools, governance, orchestration, evaluation, and execution boundaries.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell prose">
      <h2>Why it exists</h2>
      <p>
        AI systems accumulate prompts, rules, memories, tools, routing, evaluators, and automation quickly. The goal of this work is to reason about those surrounding systems as engineering—not as a collection of tricks.
      </p>

      <h2>Current themes</h2>
      <p>
        The public research covers topics such as bounded execution, explicit approval gates, artifact-based workflow transitions, behavioral evaluation, independent verification, context authority, observability boundaries, model/runtime fitness, and avoiding duplicate sources of truth.
      </p>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Scope matters.</strong> This is an evolving personal research and engineering framework, not a claim that “Harness Engineering” is a universally established discipline.
      </div>

      <p><a href="https://github.com/unyieldingclaw-dev/harness-engineering">Browse the public research repository →</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
